---
import MarketLayout from "../../../layouts/MarketLayout.astro";
import Breadcrumbs from "../../../components/Breadcrumbs.astro";
import { SITE_ORIGIN, brand, vancouver } from "../../../data/site";

const path = `${vancouver.path}/terms/`;
---

<MarketLayout
  title={`Terms of Service | ${brand.name} Metro Vancouver`}
  description={`Terms for using this site and hiring ${brand.name} in Metro Vancouver. Published mitigation rates, written rebuild scopes, and how we bill. Call ${vancouver.phoneDisplay}.`}
  path={path}
>
  <main id="main">
    <div class="wrap">
      <Breadcrumbs
        crumbs={[
          { name: brand.shortName, href: `${SITE_ORIGIN}${vancouver.path}/` },
          { name: "Terms" },
        ]}
      />
    </div>
    <section class="hero">
      <div class="wrap prose">
        <p class="eyebrow">Metro Vancouver</p>
        <h1 class="display">Terms of service</h1>
        <p class="lede">
          These terms cover this website and work we perform in Metro Vancouver. Last updated September 2026.
        </p>
        <p>
          {brand.name} operates from {vancouver.addressLine}. Emergency line: {vancouver.phoneDisplay}, 24/7.
          The site is information. A job is a contract only when you (or an authorised insurer, strata, or property
          manager) accept a written scope, or when you ask us to start emergency mitigation by phone.
        </p>

        <h2 class="display">Using this website</h2>
        <p>
          You may read, print, and share pages for personal or professional use. You may not copy the rate card,
          photographs, or copy into another restoration site, scrape the pages, or present our materials as your own.
          Prices, response times, and service descriptions can change. The live page is the current version.
        </p>
        <p>
          Area response times are worst-case targets from dispatch, not a guarantee we will be at your door in that
          minute on every call. If we cannot meet the published band, we say so when you call.
        </p>

        <h2 class="display">Emergency mitigation</h2>
        <p>
          Calling {vancouver.phoneDisplay} and asking us to attend is an instruction to start emergency mitigation:
          make the area safe, stop the source where we can, extract standing water, and set drying equipment. That work
          proceeds immediately. You do not need a signed paper on the kitchen counter before we pull water.
        </p>
        <p>
          You (or the person who called) confirm you have authority to let us into the property. If you do not own it,
          you tell us who does — strata, landlord, insurer — and we document that. We may refuse or stop work if the
          site is unsafe, if access is blocked, or if we cannot establish who is authorising the job.
        </p>

        <h2 class="display">Rates and what they cover</h2>
        <p>
          Published rates on the pricing page cover mitigation: call-out, labour, equipment, Category 3 response, and
          the demolition and treatment lines listed there. Every charge that can appear on a mitigation invoice is on
          that page. No fuel surcharge, contractor fee, or administrative add-on.
        </p>
        <p>
          Labour is rounded to the nearest half hour. Drive time is included. Equipment is billed to the day readings
          hit target, capped at five days unless you approve an extension in writing. Overnight, weekend, and statutory
          holiday labour is higher because someone has to be awake, insured, and equipped. Those bands are on the card.
        </p>
        <p>
          Rebuild — drywall, paint, flooring, millwork, finishes — is not on the mitigation card. It is quoted in
          writing after the drying log closes. We do not start rebuild until that scope is accepted.
        </p>
        <p>
          Work billed to an insurer follows that insurer's programme rates, not the public card. Work you pay yourself
          uses the published card. GST is extra.
        </p>

        <h2 class="display">Payment</h2>
        <p>
          Emergency work is due on completion. Insurer-billed files are net 30 from the closing invoice. We accept
          Interac e-Transfer, Visa, Mastercard, and cheque. We do not take a deposit on an emergency call-out.
        </p>
        <p>
          Unpaid invoices may pause remaining work, including rebuild. Collection costs reasonably incurred may be
          added. A dispute over one line does not freeze the rest of an undisputed invoice.
        </p>

        <h2 class="display">The drying log</h2>
        <p>
          We photograph the loss, take moisture readings at marked points, and keep a daily log until every point meets
          target. You can ask for that file. Surfaces can read dry while cavities do not. We do not pull equipment
          because a floor feels dry to the hand.
        </p>
        <p>
          Drying time is a range, not a promise. Three to five days is common when we extract the same night. Cool,
          damp Metro Vancouver rooms, late extraction, and Category 2 or 3 water all extend the log. If the building
          needs more than five days of equipment, we show you the readings before day six.
        </p>

        <h2 class="display">Insurance</h2>
        <p>
          We document the loss so an adjuster can read it. We do not decide what a policy pays, bind coverage, or act
          as your broker. Deductibles, depreciation, sewage-backup endorsements, and whether groundwater is excluded
          are questions for your insurer. We restore the building either way.
        </p>
        <p>
          Directing us to bill your insurer is not a guarantee they will pay. If they decline, the invoice remains
          yours unless another party has accepted it in writing.
        </p>

        <h2 class="display">Access, contents, and safety</h2>
        <p>
          You keep people and pets out of unsafe areas. You tell us about electrical hazards, sewage, and anyone who
          should not be on site. We may isolate rooms, pull baseboards, open cavities, and remove materials that cannot
          be dried. Porous goods that took Category 3 water often cannot be saved. We show you what comes out before we
          pull it where that is practical.
        </p>
        <p>
          Contents we pack out are inventoried. We are not a storage warehouse for goods left on site after we close
          the log. Salvage is not guaranteed. Pre-existing damage, hidden conditions, and water that travelled two
          rooms over before we arrived are not defects in our work.
        </p>

        <h2 class="display">Limitation</h2>
        <p>
          We perform the work with reasonable skill, in line with IICRC S500 practice as it applies to the job. We are
          not liable for loss of use, lost rent, lost stock, or other indirect loss, except where the law of British
          Columbia does not allow that limit. Our liability for a job is limited to the amount invoiced for that job,
          except in cases of our gross negligence or wilful misconduct, or death or personal injury we cause.
        </p>
        <p>
          Nothing on this site is legal, insurance, or medical advice. First-hour pages are instructions for the minutes
          before we arrive, not a substitute for calling {vancouver.phoneDisplay} while water is moving.
        </p>

        <h2 class="display">Photographs</h2>
        <p>
          Job photographs used to document a loss for you, an adjuster, or a strata are part of the file. We do not
          publish identifiable photos of your property on this site without written permission. Images currently on the
          site are illustrative.
        </p>

        <h2 class="display">Governing law</h2>
        <p>
          These terms and any job they cover are governed by the laws of British Columbia. Courts in Vancouver, BC have
          jurisdiction, except where a consumer-protection statute requires otherwise.
        </p>
        <p>
          If a part of these terms cannot be enforced, the rest still applies. We may update this page. The date at the
          top is the current version. Work already underway stays on the terms that applied when that work started,
          unless you agree otherwise in writing.
        </p>

        <h2 class="display">Contact</h2>
        <p>
          Questions about a job or this page: call {vancouver.phoneDisplay}, or write to us at {vancouver.addressLine}.
        </p>
        <p>
          <a href={`${vancouver.path}/privacy/`}>Privacy</a>
          ·
          <a href={`${vancouver.path}/pricing/`}>Published rates</a>
          ·
          <a href={`${vancouver.path}/contact/`}>Contact</a>
        </p>
      </div>
    </section>
  </main>
</MarketLayout>
